query:
My sister's birthday is coming up and she normally keeps 85-percent dark-chocolate bars around. I have about forty dollars and am torn between a small set of three origin- or cacao-percentage-labeled bars and a larger assorted truffle gift box. The bars are a Madagascar Manjari listing with conflicting 64- and 65-percent labels, a Peru 70-percent dark bar with puffed quinoa and Criollo language, and an Ecuador 60-percent dark-milk bar. The boxes are a roughly 36-piece milk, dark, white, 60-percent extra-dark, fudge-swirl and sea-salt assortment and a 16-piece milk, dark and white assortment with several flavored fillings. Do the origin and percentage labels imply real flavor differences, and which gift path would you actually choose for her?

Please audit the available evidence and these exact offers under the following boundaries:

- keep each title SKU price rating review count mass count origin percentage flavor and gift statement at frozen seller assertion scope;
- retain the Manjari listings 65 percent title and 64 percent option as an unresolved seller inconsistency;
- do not treat store ratings review counts awards premium gourmet or chocolatier wording as independent taste or recipient fit evidence;
- treat cacao percentage as composition metadata not a direct quality rank and distinguish dark milk filled and plain bar products;
- treat single origin as a location scope not a guarantee of one genotype or a predeclared flavor result;
- do not treat Criollo Forastero or Trinitario marketing labels as current botanical proof;
- preserve genetics growing conditions fermentation drying roasting recipe and processing as possible flavor axes alongside origin and percentage;
- compare bars and truffle boxes only after recording total mass piece count dark piece share shell and filling types ingredients allergens and alcohol;
- do not treat a larger box more pieces or luxury packaging as proof of better fit for an 85 percent dark chocolate recipient;
- keep each community statement at its author business product price and preference scope not as a controlled test market rate or exact offer verdict;
- verify current delivered cost stock arrival date shipping heat protection return or replacement terms and storage before purchase;
- compare cost per recipient acceptable dark serving after shipping unwanted assorted pieces and waste not sticker price or box size alone;
- run a small reversible blinded or randomized tasting of the bar candidates against the recipient's known 85 percent reference with equal portions and predeclared flavor and preference thresholds; and
- choose only the cheapest exact gift path that passes budget recipient darkness format ingredient allergen delivery and taste gates otherwise buy a small known 85 percent reference or defer.

Give me a practical research and purchase process that will:

1. build a matched exact offer price mass count origin percentage recipe rating and gift matrix;
2. separate literal seller labels and the Manjari percentage conflict from independently supported flavor inferences;
3. interpret percentage origin variety fermentation and processing evidence without turning any single label into a quality rank;
4. audit each truffle box dark piece share shells fillings ingredients allergens alcohol and recipient fit;
5. reconcile the scoped gift variety price and taste community statements;
6. verify current delivered cost availability arrival heat protection storage and remedy terms;
7. run a small matched blinded or randomized bar tasting against the recipient's known 85 percent reference; and
8. choose the cheapest exact gift path that passes all budget recipient delivery and taste gates or buy a small known 85 percent reference or defer.

Explain what percentage, origin, variety, processing, seller and community evidence can and cannot establish. Compare useful dark quantity and true delivered cost rather than presentation alone, state every material fact that remains unresolved instead of guessing it, and end with a reversible gift-selection rule I could actually follow.
